          "arguments": {
            "key": "self_review_final",
            "text": "FINAL SELF-REVIEW (v2, files_sha256 84d9c025...). Diff re-read via diff_workspace: repr.py gains _print_dict/_print_set/_print_frozenset (+15 lines); test_repr.py gains test_dict/test_set/test_frozenset (+15 lines). This is NOT comments/formatting-only: real printer methods dispatch on Python dict/set/frozenset via ReprPrinter._print.\n\nISSUE-SPECIFIC BEHAVIOR ACTUALLY EXERCISED:\n- Issue snippets: srepr({x,y}) -> wrong '{x, y}'; srepr({x:y}) -> wrong '{x: y}'. Both are builtin Python set/dict of Symbols, so they reach _print_set/_print_dict directly. Correct expected: \"{Symbol('x'), Symbol('y')}\" and \"{Symbol('x'): Symbol('y')}\".\n- My test evidence test-report-22391dd582f8fc5c4b5a1d92 (seq 53, v2): sympy -k [test_dict,test_set,test_frozenset,test_list,test_tuple] -> 6 passed, 0 errors, validation_status=verified. Those tests assert srepr({})/{x}/{x:y}/set()/frozenset()/frozenset({x}) AND eval(srepr(expr))==expr. That path is the same one the issue uses => genuinely issue-specific, not unrelated-passing.\n- sT() also enforces round-trip, so '{}' / 'set()' / 'frozenset()' / '{Symbol('x'): Symbol('y')}' are all eval-reconstructable.\n\nREGRESSION CHECK: search_repository 'srepr(set(' -> 0 matches; no existing public test asserts the old broken set/dict output, so making _print_set/_print_dict active is not a regression on repo tests. _print_dict/_print_set are new method names previously absent (verified by reading repr.py), so no previously-used dispatch is overridden.\n\nHONEST GAPS / WHAT IS NOT ESTABLISHED:\n1. Two-element set literal ({x, y}) ordering is NOT asserted anywhere — a Python set of two Symbols has nondeterministic iteration order, so upstream deliberately tested only the 1-element case. Ordering stability is therefore UNVERIFIED and arguably not assertable.\n2. Multi-key dict ordering likewise unasserted.\n3. Nested containers (e.g. srepr({x: {y}}) or set containing a list) not covered by added tests.\n4. Full test_repr.py is NOT green: 43 passed / 2 errors (test_Add, test_Mul) from pre-existing py3.9 sympify(evaluate=False) baseline breakage, unrelated to this patch but still a red full-file result.\n5. I did not run the repo-wide full suite; only printer-file scope.\n6. srepr of dict/set as arg to other printers (e.g. inside FiniteSet) not separately checked.\n\nCONCLUSION: the change genuinely implements the issue; accept stands for the covered behavior. Gaps 1-3 are test-coverage breadth, not demonstrated defects; gap 4 must be disclosed in delivery. No repair warranted from my side — code authoring/delivery is member_001's."
          }
        }
